# <APP>-SPEC-001 — <Application> product specification

## Context and intended outcome

Describe the user problem, evidence, desired outcome, and measurable signals. Distinguish known evidence from assumptions.

## Actors and permissions

Identify user, operator, administrator, external-system, and agent roles that apply. State authorization boundaries.

## Scope and exclusions

List included capabilities. List likely scope expansion and explicit exclusions with rationale.

## Functional requirements

Assign stable IDs (`FR-...`). Each requirement states actor, trigger, behavior, success, failure/empty/partial/unknown states, and testable acceptance criteria.

## Data and domain contracts

Define important entities, ownership, lifecycle, provenance, retention/deletion, validation, and source-of-truth rules. Record open data questions.

## Integrations and external boundaries

For each provider/system, state purpose, data exchanged, permissions, failure behavior, access status, and unresolved legal or commercial constraints. Do not imply an integration exists before evidence.

## Quality requirements

Use stable IDs (`NFR-...`) and define applicability and evidence for security, privacy, accessibility, performance, reliability, observability, cost, localization, and operations.

## Acceptance outcomes

Define product-level outcomes and how they will be measured. Separate app behavior acceptance from business outcome validation.

## Assumptions and dependencies

Label inference and assumptions; name owner, evidence, affected requirements, and what work is blocked.

## Open decision vectors

Use stable IDs (`D-...`). For each: unresolved question, viable options, impact, affected requirements/tasks, owner, and target decision point.

## Traceability

Link to `constitution.md`, `design.md`, `backlog.md`, decision log, research, and review records. Do not duplicate canonical content maintained in those artifacts.
